From 58f2ea77b5d7fb880b18f1657c6072b69b2b2096 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yihou <yihou@amd.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 14:53:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 23/25] docs: pack up the merge validation

The reproduction kit for the run that justifies groups C and D: two nodes, four
gates, and the two probe defects that each produced a wrong verdict before being
caught.

The gates are cumulative so a failure localises. G0 replays the kvaware+kvd
baseline with a command line byte-identical to it, so any change is attributable
to the patch set alone; G1 turns MTP on; G2 sends a prompt spanning more than one
prefill chunk; then conc=16 and conc=128.

Two numbers do the discriminating, and both are recorded with the reason they
discriminate:

  * kvd is SERVING, not merely wired. A speed-up proves nothing -- SGLang's
    in-GPU radix cache serves a repeated prefix without touching L3. Restarting
    the prefill engine empties that cache while the kvd daemon keeps running:
    102 reads afterwards with `sets` UNCHANGED and zero misses, which could only
    have come from L3.

  * The bigram fix produces the RIGHT hashes. The router view reads 51 blocks
    under MTP -- byte-identical to the plain-int path in G0, so the flattened
    keys chain to the same hashes rather than merely being non-empty. Unfixed it
    reads 0.

notes.md is the file worth reading before reproducing: the cold start that looks
like a hang, the reset ritual, the two-infera-copies trap, the router-view timing
trap, and the finding that kvd on a PD decode leg is write-only in every
configuration.

What this kit does NOT establish is stated in it three times over: at the time it
was written the merged image had never been built, and every result came from
patching the running containers. The Dockerfile argument was an argument, not a
measurement.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
